From: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 10:15:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] DwUsbHostDxe: fix the BCM2712 core reset and mode switch

Two defects keep the DWC2 OTG core behind the Pi 5's USB-C data port
from ever coming up, both inherited from the dwc_otg reference driver
this code descends from.

1. The core soft reset is ordered backwards.

DwCoreReset() waits for GRSTCTL.AHBIDLE and only then writes
GRSTCTL.CSFTRST. That deadlocks whenever the core is entered with its
AHB master mid-transfer: the reset that would return the master to idle
is exactly what the driver refuses to issue until it sees idle. Nothing
breaks the tie, so Wait4Bit() -- a busy-poll against the deadline
DwHcReset() shares between DwCoreInit() and DwHcInit() -- spends the
whole budget and the port is dead at the end of it.

Linux fixed this in commit b8ccc593eeea ("usb: dwc2: Reorder AHBIDLE and
CSFTRST in dwc2_core_reset()"), by John Youn of Synopsys:

    According to the databook, the core soft reset should be done
    before checking for AHBIDLE. The gadget version of core reset had
    it correct but the hcd version did not. This fixes the hcd version.

u-boot's dwc2_core_reset() has the same order. Ours did not.

It is reachable here because the BCM2712 DWC2 is the same core the boot
ROM drives for rpiboot, so EDK2 can inherit it with a transfer
outstanding. Observed as DwUsbHostDxe consuming the entire
DW_HC_RESET_TIMEOUT_MS budget in DriverBinding Start -- 995 ms of a
1360 ms DXE phase, and 99.5% of all driver-binding time in the
firmware, per ShellPkg's `dp`.

Note the wait for CSFTRST to self-clear stays correct: Synopsys cores
from rev 4.20a on stop self-clearing it and set CSFTRST_DONE (bit 29)
instead, but every Pi including the BCM2712 carries a pre-4.20a core --
which is also why Linux on this SoC reports the "_CSFTRST" flavour of
its soft-reset timeout and not "_CSFTRST_DONE".

2. The forced host-mode switch was not actually waited for.

The core samples as a device out of reset, and the soft reset above puts
it back there, so GUSBCFG.FORCEHOSTMODE is followed by an IDDIG debounce
before the core reports host mode. There is no OTG ID pin on the Pi 4 or
Pi 5 to do the selection -- Raspberry Pi's own "Using OTG mode on
Raspberry Pi SBCs" whitepaper says so -- which makes forcing the mode
mandatory rather than optional, and makes the settle a required step.

A fixed 25 ms sleep was both too short and unverified. Poll
GINTSTS.CURMODE for up to 110 ms instead, which is Linux's own bound in
dwc2_wait_for_mode(), and warn rather than fail on expiry exactly as
Linux does: the mode can still settle while the rest of init proceeds,
and bailing would turn a slow switch into no USB at all.

Also log the VideoCore firmware revision. On BCM2712 the DWC2 PHY is
initialised by the bootloader and by nothing else -- rpi-eeprom added it
in the 2023-12-06 release ("Initialise DWC PHY to enable DWC
host+peripheral support under Linux"), and before that Linux failed on
this very core with "dwc2_core_reset: HANG! Soft Reset timeout
GRSTCTL_CSFTRST". No driver can substitute for it; the PHY registers are
undocumented and absent from the device tree. Since the revision is a
build timestamp, printing it turns "the core answers MMIO but never
finishes its reset" into a one-line diagnosis.

Upstream-Status: Submittable [with an RPi5 platform port]
Signed-off-by: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
---
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.c
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.c
@@ -31,6 +31,15 @@
 #define DWC2_GSNPSID_ID_MASK   0xFFFF0000
 #define DWC2_GSNPSID_OTG_ID    0x4F540000
 
+/*
+ * How long the core is given to report host mode after GUSBCFG.FORCEHOSTMODE
+ * is set. Linux allows the same 110 ms (dwc2_wait_for_mode): the soft reset
+ * just before this drops the core to device mode, and the IDDIG debounce
+ * filter has to expire before it comes back. There is no OTG ID pin on the
+ * Pi 4 or Pi 5, so the mode has to be forced rather than strapped.
+ */
+#define DWC2_HOST_MODE_TIMEOUT_US  (110000)
+
   /*
    * TimerPeriodic to account for timeout processing
    * within DwHcTransfer.
@@ -219,20 +228,44 @@
 {
   EFI_STATUS Status;
 
-  Status = Wait4Bit (Timeout, DwHc->DwUsbBase + GRSTCTL, DWC2_GRSTCTL_AHBIDLE, 1);
-  if (Status) {
-    DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "DwCoreReset: AHBIDLE Timeout!\n"));
-    return Status;
-  }
-
+  //
+  // Soft reset FIRST, AHB idle after -- not the other way round.
+  //
+  // Per the Synopsys databook, and Linux commit b8ccc593eeea ("usb: dwc2:
+  // Reorder AHBIDLE and CSFTRST in dwc2_core_reset()") by John Youn of
+  // Synopsys: "the core soft reset should be done before checking for
+  // AHBIDLE. The gadget version of core reset had it correct but the hcd
+  // version did not." u-boot's dwc2_core_reset() agrees.
+  //
+  // This driver descends from the pre-fix dwc_otg reference code and kept
+  // the wrong order, which deadlocks whenever the core is entered with its
+  // AHB master mid-transfer: the reset that would return the master to
+  // idle is precisely what the driver refuses to issue until it sees idle.
+  // Nothing breaks the tie, so Wait4Bit() spends the whole shared deadline
+  // and the port is dead at the end of it. That is reachable on the Pi 5,
+  // where this same core is what the boot ROM drives for rpiboot before
+  // EDK2 ever runs.
+  //
   MmioWrite32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + GRSTCTL, DWC2_GRSTCTL_CSFTRST);
 
+  //
+  // The core clears CSFTRST itself once the reset retires. Synopsys cores
+  // from rev 4.20a on instead set GRSTCTL_CSFTRST_DONE (bit 29) and leave
+  // CSFTRST standing, but every Pi -- BCM2712 included -- carries a
+  // pre-4.20a core, so the self-clear is the correct wait here.
+  //
   Status = Wait4Bit (Timeout, DwHc->DwUsbBase + GRSTCTL, DWC2_GRSTCTL_CSFTRST, 0);
   if (Status) {
     DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "DwCoreReset: CSFTRST Timeout!\n"));
     return Status;
   }
 
+  Status = Wait4Bit (Timeout, DwHc->DwUsbBase + GRSTCTL, DWC2_GRSTCTL_AHBIDLE, 1);
+  if (Status) {
+    DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "DwCoreReset: AHBIDLE Timeout!\n"));
+    return Status;
+  }
+
   MicroSecondDelay (100000);
   return EFI_SUCCESS;
 }
@@ -1477,6 +1510,7 @@
 {
   UINT32 AhbCfg = 0;
   UINT32 UsbCfg = 0;
+  UINT32 Elapsed;
   EFI_STATUS Status;
 
   //
@@ -1527,10 +1561,45 @@
   // Pi 5 this core sits behind the USB-C connector and samples as a
   // device out of reset (the boot ROM's rpiboot gadget runs on it), so
   // without the force a connected peripheral is never seen. The Synopsys
-  // core is allowed up to 25 ms to effect a mode change.
+  // core is allowed up to 110 ms to effect a mode change.
+  //
+  // 110 ms is Linux's own bound (dwc2_wait_for_mode), and it is a debounce
+  // interval rather than a guess: the core soft reset just above drops the
+  // core back to device mode, and the IDDIG filter has to settle before it
+  // returns to host. 25 ms was optimistic. Note there is no OTG ID pin on
+  // the Pi 4 or Pi 5 -- Raspberry Pi's own OTG whitepaper says so -- which
+  // is why the mode must be forced here rather than strapped.
   //
   MmioOr32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + GUSBCFG, DWC2_GUSBCFG_FORCEHOSTMODE);
-  MicroSecondDelay (25000);
+
+  //
+  // Then wait for the core to actually say it is in host mode, rather than
+  // sleeping a fixed interval and hoping. GINTSTS.CURMODE is the core's own
+  // report. Linux polls the same bit for the same 110 ms
+  // (dwc2_wait_for_mode) and, like this, warns rather than failing if it
+  // expires -- the mode can still settle while the rest of init proceeds,
+  // and refusing to continue would turn a slow switch into no USB at all.
+  //
+  for (Elapsed = 0; Elapsed < DWC2_HOST_MODE_TIMEOUT_US; Elapsed += 1000) {
+    if ((MmioRead32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + GINTSTS) & DWC2_GINTSTS_CURMODE) ==
+        DWC2_GINTSTS_CURMODE_HOST)
+    {
+      break;
+    }
+
+    MicroSecondDelay (1000);
+  }
+
+  if (Elapsed >= DWC2_HOST_MODE_TIMEOUT_US) {
+    DEBUG ((
+      DEBUG_ERROR,
+      "DwCoreInit: core never reported host mode in %d ms (GINTSTS 0x%08x)\n",
+      DWC2_HOST_MODE_TIMEOUT_US / 1000,
+      MmioRead32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + GINTSTS)
+      ));
+  } else {
+    DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "DwCoreInit: host mode after %d ms\n", Elapsed / 1000));
+  }
 
   return EFI_SUCCESS;
 }
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DriverBinding.c
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DriverBinding.c
@@ -137,6 +137,28 @@
     DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "Couldn't power on USB: %r (continuing)\n", Status));
   }
 
+  //
+  // Report the VideoCore firmware revision, because on BCM2712 it decides
+  // whether this controller can work at all. The DWC2 PHY here is brought
+  // up by the bootloader, not by any driver -- rpi-eeprom added it in the
+  // 2023-12-06 release ("Initialise DWC PHY to enable DWC host+peripheral
+  // support under Linux"), and before that Linux failed on this same core
+  // with "dwc2_core_reset: HANG! Soft Reset timeout GRSTCTL_CSFTRST". The
+  // revision is a build timestamp, so a value predating that release is
+  // the answer to a core that answers MMIO but never finishes its reset.
+  //
+  if (mFwProtocol->GetFirmwareRevision != NULL) {
+    UINT32 FwRevision;
+
+    if (!EFI_ERROR (mFwProtocol->GetFirmwareRevision (&FwRevision))) {
+      DEBUG ((
+        DEBUG_ERROR,
+        "DwUsbHostDxe: VideoCore firmware revision 0x%08x\n",
+        FwRevision
+        ));
+    }
+  }
+
   Status = CreateDwUsbHc (&DwHc);
   if (EFI_ERROR (Status)) {
     goto Exit;
